      say: `Who's involved? Developers and engineers extend the kit, and they need PowerShell and the basics of Pester. Security officers read the results, and the catalog tells them up front what access a live run would take. A Global Administrator appears exactly once, to save the export while they're already signed in for something else. Nothing in the kit itself ever runs with that role. And when a finding needs fixing, the identity team makes the change, from the change record, under change control. The check only ever reports.`,
      detail: `Audience: developers and engineers who extend the kit, and ISSOs who want to know what a check's result can and can't be trusted to mean. You should read PowerShell and know Pester's Describe and It. The catalog's DelegatedRole names the role and the reason, so Get-VkCheckCatalog and the README tell an ISSO what running it live would require before anyone does. In Lab 2, the export is the only step that needs Global Administrator; nothing in the kit runs with that role. A Global Administrator who is already signed in for other reasons saves the GET's response to a file. The check runs on the export as any user, without signing in. The remediation (Update-MgDirectoryOnPremiseSynchronization, or Set-EntraDirSyncFeature, which Learn says needs Global Administrator) goes in the change record that the finding opens, run by the identity team under change control, never in the check.`,
