      sources: "Sources: examples/validation-kit. Chapter 03, “Lab 1: fixtures, no tenant,” steps 4–7.",
      say: `The rest of Lab 1 shows the guardrails. You ask the kit what a live run would need, and it prints the permission and names the Global Administrator role, before anyone signs in. You confirm the check isn't part of a default run. You run the kit's whole test suite. And then the best step: you deliberately break the check so it reads a property that doesn't exist. The failing fixture still fails, but now the passing one fails too, because a missing value reads as off. That's the unsafe default doing its job. Put it back, and you've seen every outcome.`,
      detail: `Step 4, see what a live run would ask for: Get-VkRequiredScope -Checks (Get-VkCheckCatalog -CheckId VK-SYNC-02) prints OnPremDirectorySynchronization.Read.All, and (Get-VkCheckCatalog -CheckId VK-SYNC-02).DelegatedRole names the role. Step 5, confirm it isn't in a default run: (Get-VkCheckCatalog).Id -contains 'VK-SYNC-02' is False. Step 6, run the whole kit's tests: pwsh -NoProfile -Command "Invoke-Pester -Path tests/powershell". Step 7, make it fail for the right reason: in your copy, change the check to read blockSoftMatch (a property that doesn't exist). The fail fixture still fails, but the pass fixture now fails too, because the missing value reads as off. That is the default-to-unsafe rule doing its job. Put it back. Expected result: every test passes, and you've seen each of the check's four outcomes: Pass, Fail, Warn and NotApplicable.`,
